+---
+name: conventional-commits
+description: 'Write clear, Conventional Commits style commit messages from a diff, staged changes or a short description of the work. Produces a typed subject line, an optional body that explains what and why, and footers for breaking changes and issue references; it also splits a mixed change into several focused commits. Use whenever the user asks for a commit message, says "commit this", "write a commit", "make a commit", wants to reword or tidy an existing message, or asks how to divide work into commits.'
+license: MIT
+compatibility: No dependencies; pure instructions, so it runs in any agent that reads SKILL.md.
+---
+
+# Conventional commits
+
+A commit message is read twice: once now by a reviewer, once later by whoever runs `git blame`. Write a one-line header anyone can scan, and add a body only when it earns its place by explaining *why*.
+
+## Format
+
+```
+<type>(<scope>): <subject>
+
+<body>
+
+<footer(s)>
+```
+
+The subject is one line. Type and scope are optional in the spec but always worth choosing deliberately; a bare `update stuff` is not a message.
+
+## Workflow
+
+1. **Read the change.** Prefer the real diff: `git diff --staged` for staged work, otherwise `git diff`. If the user only describes the work in prose, use that and state what you assumed.
+2. **Choose the type** from the table below. Pick the *intent*, not the file touched: moving code is `refactor`; adding a capability is `feat`.
+3. **Write the subject:** `type(scope): summary` — imperative, lowercase, no period, ideally ≤ 50 characters (72 is the hard ceiling).
+4. **Add a body only if it carries something the subject cannot:** the reason, the trade-off, the thing a reader would otherwise have to ask. Wrap at 72; blank line after the subject.
+5. **Add footers** for breaking changes and issue links.
+6. **If the change is really several changes**, say so and propose one commit per change instead of piling everything into one message.
+
+## Choose the type
+
+| type | Use for |
+|---|---|
+| `feat` | a new capability the user can see |
+| `fix` | a bug fix |
+| `docs` | documentation only |
+| `refactor` | a behaviour-preserving code change |
+| `perf` | a change that improves speed or memory |
+| `test` | adding or correcting tests |
+| `style` | formatting only (whitespace, semicolons) |
+| `build` | build system or dependencies |
+| `ci` | CI configuration and scripts |
+| `chore` | housekeeping that touches neither `src` nor tests |
+| `revert` | undoing a previous commit |
+
+`feat` and `fix` are the two that map to releases; use them only when that is genuinely what changed.
+
+## The subject line
+
+- **Imperative, present tense:** `add retry`, never `added`, `adds` or `adding`. It should complete "This commit will …".
+- **Lowercase after the type**, no trailing period, no `Requested by` chatter.
+- **Name the behaviour, not the diff:** `fix(auth): reject expired tokens`, not `fix: change line 42`.
+- **Keep it scannable:** aim for ≤ 50 characters; 72 is where tools start to truncate.
+- **Scope** is a noun for the affected area, lowercase, in parentheses: `parser`, `api`, `deps`. It is a hint, not a requirement — omit it when the change is repo-wide.
+
+## The body and footers
+
+- The body explains **what** changed and **why**; the diff already shows *how*.
+- One blank line between subject and body; wrap at 72; use `-` bullets for independent points.
+- Record the reasoning that is not in the code: an issue, a constraint, a rejected alternative.
+- Footers go after a blank line:
+
+```
+BREAKING CHANGE: the `token` field is now required
+Closes: #128
+Refs: #131, #140
+```
+
+A `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer, or a `!` after the type/scope (`feat(api)!: drop v1`), marks an incompatible change.
+
+## Examples
+
+Good — a feature with context:
+
+```
+feat(auth): add magic-link sign-in
+
+Email a one-time link so users can sign in without a password.
+Existing session keys are unchanged, so this is additive.
+
+Closes: #204
+```
+
+Good — a fix that names the cause:
+
+```
+fix(pagination): stop dropping the last page
+
+The cursor was compared with `<` instead of `<=`, so the final
+partial page was never returned. Add a test for the odd-count case.
+```
+
+Good — one-liners:
+
+```
+docs(readme): document the skills install command
+refactor(parser): extract the token reader into its own module
+test(rate-limit): cover the burst boundary
+chore: bump pytest to 8.3
+```
+
+Avoid:
+
+```
+update stuff
+fixed bug
+WIP
+Added a new feature and also fixed the login and refactored css
+```
+
+The first three say nothing. The last hides three unrelated changes that belong in three commits.
+
+## Rules
+
+- One logical change per commit. If the subject needs "and", split it.
+- Do not invent a type or scope the project has not defined; if the repo already has its own convention, follow that instead and say so.
+- Describe the effect, not the file (`document the install command`, not `change SKILL.md`).
+- Match the language and wording style of the existing log.
+- Output the message ready to paste. Do not commit, push or reword history unless the user explicitly asks.
